(ned TCP CAL -- park after the second touch, operator 2026-08-05:)
("it goes back to a safe distance, A = 0, and returns to dead center above)
(probe, 10mm above the probe".)
(ORDER MATTERS. Z clears FIRST, then the puck drops, then A unwinds: at)
(A=0 the tool tip sits lower than at any tilt, so unwinding A while low)
(would drive the needle down onto the puck.)
(XY COMES FROM G30, #5181/#5182, IN MACHINE COORDINATES. The stored puck)
(centre #3045/#3046 is written from #5061/#5062, so it is a WORK-frame)
(number and is only valid in the frame that was active when the puck was)
(measured. Today those two numbers equal G30 exactly, which means they)
(were taken with a zero offset -- and G54 is offset by tens of mm now, so)
(a G90 move to them would park somewhere else entirely. G30 holds the)
(same XY in machine terms and cannot drift with the work offset.)
(Z stays in the WORK frame, referenced to the touch-1 contact: this)
(needle own reading of the puck face. #3047 was measured with a different)
(tool, so its number only means the puck top for THAT stickout.)
(Args: #1 = touch-1 contact Z, work frame -- the puck top for THIS tool)
(      #2 = absolute work-frame safe Z for the A unwind)
o<tcp_park> sub
  #<ztop>  = #1
  #<zsafe> = #2
  #<trav>  = 2000.0
  #<lift>  = 1000.0

  o10 if [#<_metric> EQ 0]
    (abort, TCP CAL: switch to MM first - this routine is metric)
  o10 endif

  G90
  G1 Z#<zsafe> F[#<lift>]
  ; puck down before anything traverses -- an energised solenoid bleeds the
  ; air supply down, learned 2026-08-02 the hard way
  M65 P3
  G4 P1.5
  G1 A0 F1000
  ; dead centre over the probe in MACHINE coordinates, no Z word, so the
  ; clearance established above is held right through the traverse
  G53 G1 X#5181 Y#5182 F[#<trav>]
  G1 Z[#<ztop> + 10.0] F[#<lift>]
o<tcp_park> endsub
M2
